<h1>Cairn Privacy Policy</h1>
<p class="note"><em>Last updated: <%= lastUpdated %></em></p>

<p class="lede mt-14"><strong>Cairn</strong> ("the app") is developed by
  <strong>CappyLabs</strong> ("we", "us"). Cairn is a private, self-hostable
  location-sharing app. This policy explains what data Cairn handles, how, and your
  choices. Cairn is built to <strong>minimise</strong> the data we can see — your
  location is end-to-end encrypted and we cannot read it.</p>
<% if (typeof controller !== 'undefined' && controller) { %>
<p class="note">The data controller for the hosted Cairn service is
  <strong><%= controller %></strong>. Contact:
  <a href="mailto:<%= privacyEmail %>"><%= privacyEmail %></a>.</p>
<% } %>

<h2>Our core principle</h2>
<div class="card accent">
  <p class="note">Cairn has <strong>no traditional accounts</strong> — no email, phone
    number, name, or password is required. Your identity is a cryptographic key
    generated on your device. Your location is encrypted on your device and can only be
    read by the specific people you choose to share with.
    <strong>We (and the server) cannot decrypt your location.</strong></p>
</div>

<h2>What you share with your contacts</h2>
<div class="card">
  <p class="note">End-to-end encrypted on your device, separately for each contact you
    have paired with. The server stores only ciphertext it cannot read.</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Your location</strong> — precise, or rounded to about 1 km, as you choose for each contact (or not at all). Shared only while you are sharing: while the app is open, or in the background if you turn that on.</li>
    <li><strong>Your display name</strong>, if you set one.</li>
    <li><strong>A status</strong> you choose to show (for example "Hotel"), or the name of one of your places you are in, if you've made that place visible to contacts.</li>
    <li><strong>Your battery level</strong> and whether you're charging — on by default; you can turn it off.</li>
    <li><strong>Your speed and direction of travel</strong> — off by default; only if you turn them on, and only with precise sharing.</li>
    <li><strong>Pins</strong> you choose to share with specific contacts (a name, a point and an optional note).</li>
  </ul>
</div>

<h2>What's stored for you alone</h2>
<div class="card">
  <p class="note">Encrypted to your own key, so only you can read it — on any device
    where you restore your recovery phrase.</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Location history</strong> — only if you agree to the server's history policy when asked, which tells you how long the server keeps it. It records your own trail and, by default, the locations your contacts share with you; you can turn this off for each contact. You can also keep less than the server does, or clear it.</li>
    <li><strong>Your places</strong> (for example Home or Work) — their names, locations and size, used for arrive/leave alerts.</li>
  </ul>
</div>

<h2>What the server can see</h2>
<div class="card">
  <p class="note">To deliver encrypted updates, the server necessarily handles some
    information it can read:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Device identity</strong> — your public cryptographic key. This is not linked to your real-world identity unless you choose to reveal it.</li>
    <li><strong>Connections</strong> — which devices you are paired with, and how precisely you share with each (precise, approximate or paused).</li>
    <li><strong>Timing</strong> — who sends encrypted updates to whom and when, when you were last active, and, for your stored history, which days it covers and whose it is (yours or a contact's), but not what's in it.</li>
    <li><strong>Pairing requests</strong> — who asked to pair with whom.</li>
    <li><strong>Your IP address</strong>, as with any internet service, which may appear in server logs.</li>
  </ul>
</div>

<h2>What stays on your device</h2>
<div class="card">
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Nicknames</strong> you give contacts, and your per-contact settings.</li>
    <li><strong>Camera</strong> — used only to scan a pairing QR code. Images are not stored or transmitted.</li>
    <li><strong>Activity sensor</strong> — off unless you turn it on. Tags your own history with how you travelled (walking, cycling, in a vehicle); it leaves your device only inside your encrypted history.</li>
    <li><strong>Notifications</strong> — made on your device; their content is never sent to us.</li>
  </ul>
</div>

<h2>Other services the app uses</h2>
<div class="card">
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Map tiles from Esri (ArcGIS Online).</strong> To draw the map, the app downloads map images for the area you're viewing. Esri receives your IP address and which area of the map is on screen, but not your shared location or anyone else's.</li>
    <li><strong>Snap to roads (optional).</strong> If you ask the app to snap a trip in your History to the roads, and agree when asked, that trip's coordinates are sent to the Cairn server's own router, which doesn't store them. If that router isn't available, they're sent instead to a public router at routing.openstreetmap.de, run by FOSSGIS e.V. This is the only time locations leave the app without end-to-end encryption.</li>
    <li><strong>Google Play</strong> — on Android, the app asks Google Play whether a newer version is available.</li>
  </ul>
</div>

<h2>What we do NOT do</h2>
<div class="card">
  <ul>
    <li>We do <strong>not</strong> sell your data.</li>
    <li>We do <strong>not</strong> use advertising or third-party analytics/tracking SDKs.</li>
    <li>Apart from the services listed above, we do <strong>not</strong> share your data with third parties. Your location is shared only with the contacts you pair with, end-to-end encrypted.</li>
    <li>We <strong>cannot</strong> read your location content at any time.</li>
  </ul>
</div>

<h2>How the data is used</h2>
<div class="card">
  <p class="note">Solely to provide the app's function: letting you share your live
    location with the specific people you choose, showing theirs to you, and the
    features you turn on (history, places and alerts, shared pins). There is no
    secondary use.</p>
</div>

<h2>Legal basis &amp; your rights (UK/EU GDPR)</h2>
<div class="card">
  <p class="note">We process the limited data above to perform the service you request
    (and, for location and location history, on the basis of your consent, which you can
    withdraw at any time by turning off sharing or history, or deleting your identity).
    You have the right to access, correct, and delete your data (see below), and to
    complain to your data protection authority (in the UK, the ICO).</p>
</div>

<h2>Where your data is stored</h2>
<div class="card">
  <p class="note">The hosted Cairn server at cairn.cappylabs.uk is located in the UK.
    If you use your own server, your data is stored wherever you run it.</p>
</div>

<h2>Data retention and deletion</h2>
<div class="card">
  <p class="note">You can delete your identity and all associated data at any time:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>In the app:</strong> menu (⋮) → Backup &amp; restore → Danger zone → <strong>Delete this identity</strong>. This is immediate and permanent.</li>
    <li><strong>By request:</strong> see <a href="/delete">cairn.cappylabs.uk/delete</a>.</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="note mt-12">Deleting your identity removes your identity, connections,
    encrypted location shares, location history, places, shared pins (yours, and copies
    shared with you), pairing requests and presence. We do not retain personal data tied
    to your identity after deletion. Location history is otherwise kept for the period
    shown in the app when you agree to it, then deleted automatically by the server.
    Routine server security logs (which do not contain location content) may persist up
    to 30 days, then are purged.</p>
</div>

<h2>Self-hosting</h2>
<div class="card">
  <p class="note">Cairn is self-hostable. If you connect the app to your own server,
    your data resides only on your server and CappyLabs receives nothing. The map tiles,
    the public snap-to-roads fallback and Google Play described above still apply.</p>
</div>

<h2>Security</h2>
<div class="card">
  <p class="note">Location content is end-to-end encrypted (X25519 key agreement with
    ChaCha20-Poly1305). Private keys never leave your device. Traffic to the server is
    protected with HTTPS. See <a href="/security">how Cairn's security works</a>.</p>
</div>

<h2>Children</h2>
<div class="card">
  <p class="note">Cairn is not directed to children under 13, and we do not knowingly
    collect data from them.</p>
</div>

<h2>Changes to this policy</h2>
<div class="card">
  <p class="note">We may update this policy; material changes will be reflected by the
    "Last updated" date above.</p>
</div>

<h2>Contact</h2>
<p>CappyLabs — <a href="mailto:<%= privacyEmail %>"><%= privacyEmail %></a></p>
